  "query_preview": "..(This Evening Through Sunday)\nIssued at 1249 PM MDT Sun Oct 11 2026\n\nUpper level analysis and water vapor imagery show closed upper \nlow over northeastern OR/southeastern WA with trough axis \nextending southward. Broad upper level ascent continues to \noverspread the Northern Plains as moisture streams into the \nregion. The surface cold front settled south of the forecast area \nand stretches from upper MN through southeastern SD into NE. \nConsiderable cloud cover persists over the forecast area; have \ndropped high temperatures to account with most areas (especially \nalong and north of I90) struggling to push out of the 50s today. \nIncreasing chances of rain later this afternoon through the \novernight hours as better upper level ascent overspreads the area,\nalthough heaviest precipitation amounts will remain north of the \nforecast area.\n\nWinds will turn northwesterly Monday with gusts around 40 mph across \nthe western SD plains as secondary front pushes south of the area. \nUpper level low will slowly eject from the Four Corners region late \nTuesday into Wednesday with additional precipitation chances, \nalthough better chances of appreciable precipitation amounts will\nremain south of the CWA. Cooler air will linger through the week \nwith a threat of near-freezing to freezing temperatures possible \neach night Monday night through the week.\n\nThe NBM continues to show wind gusts exceeding 60 mph across \nnorthwestern SD and portions of the western SD plains Thursday \nafternoon and evening. However, ensemble guidance is not supportive, \nwith low probabilities of high winds (EPS generally ~40-50% \nprobability of exceeding 40 mph) and many members (EPS/GEFS) peaking \nwell below 45 mph. Current thinking is that the most favorable \ningredients and strongest winds will remain displaced to our east, \nalthough confidence is low given the large ensemble spreads.\n\nUpper ridge is forecast to build over the western US by the end of \nthe week into next weekend with temperatures trending above average",
